{"id":"1343c742-b075-4029-9097-02d339b9c5a5","object":"chat.completion","created":1784987444,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    tokens = case[\\\"tokens\\\"]\\n    calls = case[\\\"calls\\\"]\\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\\\"score\\\"], c[\\\"start\\\"], c[\\\"call_id\\\"]))\\n    selected = []\\n    intervals = []\\n    for call in sorted_calls:\\n        s = call[\\\"start\\\"]\\n        e = call[\\\"end\\\"]\\n        overlap = False\\n        for (si, ei) in intervals:\\n            if max(s, si) < min(e, ei):\\n                overlap = True\\n                break\\n        if not overlap:\\n            selected.append(call)\\n            intervals.append((s, e))\\n    selected.sort(key=lambda c: c[\\\"start\\\"])\\n    selected_call_ids = [call[\\\"call_id\\\"] for call in selected]\\n    result_tokens = tokens[:]\\n    for call in sorted(selected, key=lambda c: -c[\\\"end\\\"]):\\n        s = call[\\\"start\\\"]\\n        e = call[\\\"end\\\"]\\n        replacement = call[\\\"replacement_tokens\\\"]\\n        result_tokens[s:e] = replacement\\n    return {\\\"selected_call_ids\\\": selected_call_ids, \\\"tokens\\\": result_tokens}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for AGENT-TF-02. The task is to apply accepted API calls to text (tokens) while preserving registered span order, rejecting overlaps, and keeping all eligible calls. The input case has \"tokens\" (list of strings) and \"calls\" (list of objects with call_id, start, end, score, replacement_tokens). We need to select calls: rank by descending score, then start position, then call ID (presumably ascending? The description says \"Rank candidates by descending score, start position, then call ID.\" Usually that means primary key descending score, then ascending start? But \"then call ID\" likely ascending alphabetically? Should check typical behavior. Then greedily accept calls that do not overlap already selected spans. Overlap means intervals intersect? Probably any overlap (including touching?) We need to reject overlaps. Usually, if two spans overlap in any way (start < other's end and end > other's start), reject the later one. Then order accepted calls by source start for reporting. Then apply replacements from right to left so earlier offsets do not shift. Return selected_call_ids (list of call IDs in the order they were applied? Actually \"Order accepted calls by source start for reporting.\" So the selected_call_ids should be sorted by the start index (ascending). The tokens output: the interleaved token stream after applying replacements. Apply replacements from right to left to avoid offset shifts. That means we start from the highest end index, apply replacement, and move left. But we need to compute the final token list.\n\nProcedure:\n1. Validate input: case has \"tokens\" (list), \"calls\" (list). Each call has call_id, start, end, score, replacement_tokens.\n2. Sort calls by criteria: descending score, then ascending start? The phrase \"Rank candidates by descending score, start position, then call ID.\" Typically, if scores equal, sort by start ascending, then call ID ascending (since call ID is string, alphabetical). But need to confirm from examples? There are three identical cases in fixtures, all same scores, same starts. That won't distinguish. But we follow the rule.\n3. Greedy selection: iterate over sorted calls, keep a list of selected intervals. For each call, check if its interval [start, end) (or inclusive? start and end are indices? The tokens list indices. start inclusive, end exclusive? Typically in such tasks, start and end are indices where the span covers tokens from start to end-1 inclusive. For example, call a: start=1, end=3 -> tokens[1], tokens[2] are replaced. Call b: start=2, end=5 -> tokens[2], tokens[3], tokens[4]. Overlap because both include token[2]. So reject b if a already selected. We'll treat intervals as [start, end). Overlap if max(start1, start2) < min(end1, end2). Also check end > start because valid span.\n4. After selection, sort selected calls by start (ascending) for reporting order. That sorted list is selected_call_ids in that order.\n5. For token interleaving: we have original tokens list. We need to replace spans with replacement_tokens. Do it from right to left to avoid index shifting. So create a list of replacements: for each selected call (in original selection order? Since we need to apply from right to left, we sort selected calls by start descending (largest start first) to apply replacements. Then for each replacement, we replace tokens[ start : end ] with the replacement_tokens list. This will produce final token list.\n6. Return {\"selected_call_ids\": list of call_ids in start order, \"tokens\": final token list}.\n\nEdge cases: no overlaps, empty calls, etc.\n\nNow produce Python source for solve(case) returning dict.\n\nNote: The output schema requires \"tokens\" list of strings, \"selected_call_ids\" list of strings.\n\nWe'll implement inside function.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Use standard library only.\n- Do not mutate case.\n- Parse case fields as needed.\n\nWrite solve(case): takes a dict.\n\nLet's write code.\n\nWe'll also include docstring.\n\nNow produce JSON with key \"implementation\" and value as escaped string of Python code.\n\nWe must ensure no other output.\n\nLet's write the Python code first, then escape for JSON.\n\nPython code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    tokens = case[\"tokens\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    # Validate? Not explicitly required but we can assume valid.\n    # Sort candidates by descending score, ascending start, ascending call_id\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\"score\"], c[\"start\"], c[\"call_id\"]))\n    selected = []\n    intervals = []  # list of (start, end) for selected calls\n    for call in sorted_calls:\n        s = call[\"start\"]\n        e = call[\"end\"]\n        # Check overlap with any selected interval\n        overlap = False\n        for (si, ei) in intervals:\n            if max(s, si) < min(e, ei):  # strict overlap (if equal, not overlap? usually they should be disjoint)\n                overlap = True\n                break\n        if not overlap:\n            selected.append(call)\n            intervals.append((s, e))\n    # Order selected calls by start for reporting\n    selected.sort(key=lambda c: c[\"start\"])\n    selected_call_ids = [call[\"call_id\"] for call in selected]\n    # Apply replacements from right to left\n    # Create a copy of tokens list to modify\n    result_tokens = tokens[:]\n    # Sort selected calls by end descending to apply right-to-left\n    for call in sorted(selected, key=lambda c: -c[\"end\"]):\n        s = call[\"start\"]\n        e = call[\"end\"]\n        replacement = call[\"replacement_tokens\"]\n        result_tokens[s:e] = replacement\n    return {\n        \"selected_call_ids\": selected_call_ids,\n        \"tokens\": result_tokens\n    }\n```\n\nCheck overlap condition: if intervals [1,3) and [2,5) overlap because max(1,2)=2 < min(3,5)=3 -> overlap. If intervals are [1,3) and [3,5) then max=3 < min=3? No, 3 < 3 is false, so no overlap. So allowed. That matches typical non-overlapping.\n\nNow we need to wrap this in JSON with key \"implementation\". We'll escape the Python string: replace backslashes, quotes, newlines.\n\nWe'll generate the JSON manually or with Python. Since we are to output directly, we need to be careful.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    tokens = case[\\\"tokens\\\"]\\n    calls = case[\\\"calls\\\"]\\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\\\"score\\\"], c[\\\"start\\\"], c[\\\"call_id\\\"]))\\n    selected = []\\n    intervals = []\\n    for call in sorted_calls:\\n        s = call[\\\"start\\\"]\\n        e = call[\\\"end\\\"]\\n        overlap = False\\n        for (si, ei) in intervals:\\n            if max(s, si) < min(e, ei):\\n                overlap = True\\n                break\\n        if not overlap:\\n            selected.append(call)\\n            intervals.append((s, e))\\n    selected.sort(key=lambda c: c[\\\"start\\\"])\\n    selected_call_ids = [call[\\\"call_id\\\"] for call in selected]\\n    result_tokens = tokens[:]\\n    for call in sorted(selected, key=lambda c: -c[\\\"end\\\"]):\\n        s = call[\\\"start\\\"]\\n        e = call[\\\"end\\\"]\\n        replacement = call[\\\"replacement_tokens\\\"]\\n        result_tokens[s:e] = replacement\\n    return {\\\"selected_call_ids\\\": selected_call_ids, \\\"tokens\\\": result_tokens}\"\n}\n\nBut we need to escape newlines and quotes inside the string. The JSON string value must have escaped newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, double quotes as \\\". Let's do it properly.\n\nWe can write a small Python script to generate the escaped string. Since we are not executing, we can manually do it.\n\nOriginal Python code string (without quotes around outer). Let's write it with proper indentation.\n\nBetter to use triple quotes? But in JSON, we need to escape newlines.\n\nLet's produce the escaped string:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\\\n    tokens = case[\\\\\\\"tokens\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    calls = case[\\\\\\\"calls\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\\\\\\\"score\\\\\\\"], c[\\\\\\\"start\\\\\\\"], c[\\\\\\\"call_id\\\\\\\"]))\\\\n    selected = []\\\\n    intervals = []\\\\n    for call in sorted_calls:\\\\n        s = call[\\\\\\\"start\\\\\\\"]\\\\n        e = call[\\\\\\\"end\\\\\\\"]\\\\n        overlap = False\\\\n        for (si, ei) in intervals:\\\\n            if max(s, si) < min(e, ei):\\\\n                overlap = True\\\\n                break\\\\n        if not overlap:\\\\n            selected.append(call)\\\\n            intervals.append((s, e))\\\\n    selected.sort(key=lambda c: c[\\\\\\\"start\\\\\\\"]) \\\\n    selected_call_ids = [call[\\\\\\\"call_id\\\\\\\"] for call in selected]\\\\n    result_tokens = tokens[:]\\\\n    for call in sorted(selected, key=lambda c: -c[\\\\\\\"end\\\\\\\"]):\\\\n        s = call[\\\\\\\"start\\\\\\\"]\\\\n        e = call[\\\\\\\"end\\\\\\\"]\\\\n        replacement = call[\\\\\\\"replacement_tokens\\\\\\\"]\\\\n        result_tokens[s:e] = replacement\\\\n    return {\\\\\\\"selected_call_ids\\\\\\\": selected_call_ids, \\\\\\\"tokens\\\\\\\": result_tokens}\"\n\nBut note: in JSON, backslash must be doubled. So double all backslashes? Actually in the string value, backslash is escaped as \\\\, so to represent a single backslash in the Python string, we need \\\\\\\\ in JSON. For newline, we use \\n (backslash then n). For double quote inside, we use \\\". Let's carefully build.\n\nWe can use a Python script to generate the correct JSON. But since we are the AI, we can write the JSON directly. I'll write a Python script mentally and produce output.\n\nLet's do step by step.\n\nThe Python code as a string (with actual newlines) is:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    tokens = case[\"tokens\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\"score\"], c[\"start\"], c[\"call_id\"]))\n    selected = []\n    intervals = []\n    for call in sorted_calls:\n        s = call[\"start\"]\n        e = call[\"end\"]\n        overlap = False\n        for (si, ei) in intervals:\n            if max(s, si) < min(e, ei):\n                overlap = True\n                break\n        if not overlap:\n            selected.append(call)\n            intervals.append((s, e))\n    selected.sort(key=lambda c: c[\"start\"])\n    selected_call_ids = [call[\"call_id\"] for call in selected]\n    result_tokens = tokens[:]\n    for call in sorted(selected, key=lambda c: -c[\"end\"]):\n        s = call[\"start\"]\n        e = call[\"end\"]\n        replacement = call[\"replacement_tokens\"]\n        result_tokens[s:e] = replacement\n    return {\"selected_call_ids\": selected_call_ids, \"tokens\": result_tokens}\n```\n\nNow escape for JSON:\n- Replace \\ with \\\\\n- Replace \" with \\\"\n- Replace newline with \\n (and we need to ensure no literal newline in the JSON string).\n\nSo we replace each line and join with \\n.\n\nLet's write it as a single line with \\n.\n\nI'll manually escape:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    tokens = case[\\\"tokens\\\"];\\n    calls = case[\\\"calls\\\"];\\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\\\"score\\\"], c[\\\"start\\\"], c[\\\"call_id\\\"]));\\n    selected = [];\\n    intervals = [];\\n    for call in sorted_calls:\\n        s = call[\\\"start\\\"];\\n        e = call[\\\"end\\\"];\\n        overlap = False;\\n        for (si, ei) in intervals:\\n            if max(s, si) < min(e, ei):\\n                overlap = True;\\n                break;\\n        if not overlap:\\n            selected.append(call);\\n            intervals.append((s, e));\\n    selected.sort(key=lambda c: c[\\\"start\\\"]);\\n    selected_call_ids = [call[\\\"call_id\\\"] for call in selected];\\n    result_tokens = tokens[:];\\n    for call in sorted(selected, key=lambda c: -c[\\\"end\\\"]):\\n        s = call[\\\"start\\\"];\\n        e = call[\\\"end\\\"];\\n        replacement = call[\\\"replacement_tokens\\\"];\\n        result_tokens[s:e] = replacement;\\n    return {\\\"selected_call_ids\\\": selected_call_ids, \\\"tokens\\\": result_tokens}\"\n\nBut note: I added semicolons? Not needed but okay. Actually Python doesn't need semicolons; they are optional. Remove them to be clean. Also careful with indentation: four spaces. In JSON string, we need to keep spaces.\n\nBetter to produce exactly as above without extra semicolons.\n\nI'll generate the escaped string accurately.\n\nLet's produce final JSON.\n\nWe need to output exactly one JSON object: {\"implementation\": \"<escaped code>\"}\n\nI'll write that now.\n\nMake sure no trailing newline or spaces.\n\nI'll produce the JSON string."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1539,"completion_tokens":3370,"total_tokens":4909,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1536},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3064},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1536,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":3},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}